WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.079 --> 00:00:06.160
The R-rated Logan rips, wrenches, and roars
its way through its 137-minute run-time.

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:08.530
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, it's Jan here!

00:00:08.530 --> 00:00:13.950
Thanks to the success of Ryan Reynolds' brilliant
R-rated Deadpool, we now have Logan, one of

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:18.970
the best ever X-Men movies and definitely
the very best Wolverine movie, a film that

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:21.970
both the awesome Hugh Jackman and comic book
fans deserve.

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:26.859
Yes, the year in celluloid superheroes started
out great with the fun-loving parody that

00:00:26.859 --> 00:00:33.150
was The Lego Batman Movie, but standing, snarling
180-degrees away from that film's tone is

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:37.440
Logan, which takes the high bar set by Lego
Batman, tears it to shreds, and puts another

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:39.350
even higher bar in its place.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:44.330
Star Hugh Jackman and director James Mangold,
who already greatly improved on the X-Man's

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:48.869
first solo outing with The Wolverine, promised
something different with this new movie and

00:00:48.869 --> 00:00:49.960
they weren't kidding.

00:00:49.960 --> 00:00:54.370
Mangold and his co-writers Michael Green and
Scott Frank have successfully ripped up the

00:00:54.370 --> 00:01:00.010
comic book movie playbook and gone for a full-fat,
dark and moody western, that's ravishingly

00:01:00.010 --> 00:01:05.360
shot in all its rusty, dusty glory by Gladiator
cinematographer John Mathieson, and effectively

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:07.350
scored by Marco Beltrami.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:11.670
Of course, director Mangold's got form in
the western genre, having brought an excellent

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:15.360
remake of the classic 3:10 to Yuma to the
big screen.

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:19.350
And while Logan channels the likes of Clint
Eastwood's Oscar-winning Unforgiven, George

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:23.680
Miller's Mad Max series, as well as Hollywood's
Golden Age of Westerns, it also wears the

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:27.340
influence of the Man in Black, Johnny Cash,
firmly on its sleeve.

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:31.600
Which is hardly surprising, given that Mangold
was the director who gave us the Cash biopic

00:01:31.600 --> 00:01:33.060
Walk The Line.

00:01:33.060 --> 00:01:37.390
And Mangold's Logan movie doesn't waste time
when it comes to getting its adamantium claws

00:01:37.390 --> 00:01:38.390
dirty.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:42.270
In fact, it dives head first straight into
the kind of action you can expect throughout;

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:49.201
forget quick cuts away, this action is visibly
brutal, bloody, and 100% full-on, and certainly

00:01:49.201 --> 00:01:50.201
earns its R rating.

00:01:50.201 --> 00:01:54.490
Indeed, from start to finish, the whole film
is filled with WTF moments.

00:01:54.490 --> 00:01:59.860
Props go to Logan's stunt team for numerous
incredible fight scenes, each one more flinch-inducing

00:01:59.860 --> 00:02:04.810
and crazy than the last, yet each of which
feels so raw and authentic that they cleverly

00:02:04.810 --> 00:02:08.690
hide the hard work that's gone into choreographing
and executing them.

00:02:08.690 --> 00:02:12.290
And the movie's sound department have outdone
themselves too, especially when it comes to

00:02:12.290 --> 00:02:16.890
the action scenes as the sound of those mutant
claws ripping and piercing through everything

00:02:16.890 --> 00:02:20.090
in sight is spot-on as it's just perfectly
excruciating!

00:02:20.090 --> 00:02:25.370
Plus there's some horrifyingly good visual
effects work from the movie's VFX team.

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:27.990
This Logan is untamed and untrammelled.

00:02:27.990 --> 00:02:31.550
He may be totally vicious, but he's also scarred
and hurting badly.

00:02:31.550 --> 00:02:35.330
We've not seen him quite like this in the
X-Men movies before.

00:02:35.330 --> 00:02:40.280
And Hugh Jackman gives a pitch-perfect performance,
really making you feel just how worn-out,

00:02:40.280 --> 00:02:43.069
run-down, and weary this Wolverine really
is.

00:02:43.069 --> 00:02:47.240
And Dafne Keen may be a newcomer to the big
screen but as Laura, she matches Jackman's

00:02:47.240 --> 00:02:49.280
superlative performance throughout.

00:02:49.280 --> 00:02:51.130
She really is that good.

00:02:51.130 --> 00:02:55.480
The way she moves, the way she attacks, the
way she eats, and the way she communicates,

00:02:55.480 --> 00:02:59.849
all leave you in absolutely no doubt just
how feral her character is.

00:02:59.849 --> 00:03:03.760
Add to that the fact that Keen is as good
at playing the humour and tender moments as

00:03:03.760 --> 00:03:08.480
well as the more physical scenes, and we have
a real potential future star in our midst.

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:12.840
Actually, the quality of her performance reminds
me of the first time I saw Hailee Steinfeld

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:17.209
on the big screen in the Coen Brothers' True
Grit and also Millie Bobby Brown as Eleven

00:03:17.209 --> 00:03:18.849
in Stranger Things.

00:03:18.849 --> 00:03:22.739
As for Patrick Stewart as a 90-year-old Professor
X, what can I say?

00:03:22.739 --> 00:03:26.890
Except that, just like Jackman and Keen, he's
exceptional, wringing out every last drop

00:03:26.890 --> 00:03:29.470
of anguish, heart, and humour from the script.

00:03:29.470 --> 00:03:33.989
Speaking of humour, Stephen Merchant brings
a nice dose of that to the film as the mutant-tracking

00:03:33.989 --> 00:03:36.410
Caliban, a part he plays superbly.

00:03:36.410 --> 00:03:40.510
While Boyd Holbrook puts in an effectively
nasty turn as Donald Pierce, who's out to

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:45.330
get Laura, along with Richard E. Grant's evil
scientist, who unfortunately doesn't get a

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:47.650
great deal to make a particular impression
with.

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:52.620
To sum up: James Mangold's Logan is a bold
and satisfying cinematic take on the much-loved

00:03:52.620 --> 00:03:53.870
X-Men character.

00:03:53.870 --> 00:03:58.530
In his final outing donning those adamantium
claws, Hugh Jackman skilfully steps up to

00:03:58.530 --> 00:04:02.680
the challenge of creating a character and
a film that will likely be held up as benchmarks

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.530
for future comic-book to celluloid adaptations.

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:09.940
While Patrick Stewart and Dafne Keen join
Jackman in giving stand-out turns in a film

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:13.500
that never shies away from the nasty realities
of its world.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.660
Make sure you see Logan on the big screen
as soon as you can.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.180
As for me, I'll definitely be watching it
again.

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:23.039
Now, what are you looking forward to about
Logan, or if you've seen it, what did you

00:04:23.039 --> 00:04:24.039
think of it?

00:04:24.039 --> 00:04:28.020
And what other comic book characters would
you like to see get the R-rated treatment?

